--- Exercise 37: Insertion sort algorithm
fmod INSERT-SORT is
  protecting LIST-INT .
  
  op insertSort : List -> List .
  op insert : Int List -> List .
  
  vars I J : Int .
  vars L : List .

  eq insert(I, nil) = I .
  eq insert(I, J L) = if I <= J then I J L else J insert(I, L) fi .

  eq insertSort(nil) = nil .
  eq insertSort(I) = I .
  eq insertSort(I L) = insert(I, insertSort(L)) .

endfm

--- Some more testing of my own
red insertSort(nil) .   --- nil
red insertSort(1) .     --- 1
red insertSort(1 2) .   --- 1 2
red insertSort(2 1) .   --- 1 2

red insertSort(1 2 3) .   --- 1 2 3
red insertSort(1 3 2) .   --- 1 2 3
red insertSort(2 1 3) .   --- 1 2 3
red insertSort(2 3 1) .   --- 1 2 3
red insertSort(3 1 2) .   --- 1 2 3
red insertSort(3 2 1) .   --- 1 2 3



--- Exercise 63.1-4
--- 1. Here we use lpo-1. For each input in left side we check if it is greater than
---    or equal to the right side, if any is then it terminates.

---    As you can see on the left function we have t_i = f(x)
---    and on the right side we have u = f(x).
---    Thus, we have f(x) = f(x) -> t_i = u.

--- 2. In this case we use lpo-2. That is because we compare two different functions.
---    If we know the order we can show the termination using lpo-2.

--- 3. Lpo-2 is probably the choice here. If we know that {f > g} and
---    {f(g(x)) > g(x)} then we can show that {f(g(x)) > g(f(x))}. 

--- 4. This is a very similar situation to task 3 in which we also use lpo-2.

--- Exercise 71.1-2
--- 1. from {f(g(x)) = h(x), h(x) = g(f(x)), a = b} we can assign weights to these
---    functions. Assume that we have a function {W(x)} that tells us the weight
---    of a function. Now we lets generate some pseudo-random weights for each one
---    {W(f(x)) = 2 * W(x) + 1}, {W(g(x)) = W(x) + 2}, {W(h(x)) = 2 * W(x) + 4}.

---    From this we can now show that the weight strictly decreases.
---    1. {W(f(g(x))) = 2 * W(x) + 5}, and since {W(h(x)) = 2 * W(x) + 4} we get
---       {W(f(g(x))) > W(h(x))} meaning that it is decreasing in step 1.

---    2. {W(g(f(x))) = 2 * W(x) + 3}, and again since {W(h(x)) = 2 * W(x) + 4}
---       we get that {h(x) > W(g(f(x))} which shows that it is decreasing again.

---    Thus we have shown that it is possible to prove by providing an example.
---    In the end we got that the weights can be {f(g(x)) > h(x) > g(f(x))}.

--- 2. It is not possible to prove the termination of this specification.
---    If we attempt to do so we will encounter a precedence errors.

---    1. Lets assume that we have a precendence {f > h > g}. According to the
---       precedence {f(g(x)) > h(x)} which is fine. However the next step isn't.
---       That is because {h(x) > g(f(x))}, and if we check using lpo-2 we get
---       {x > f(x)} which simply contradicts the fact that {f(x) > x}.

---    2. if we attempt to do this with other precedences we will run into similar
---       problem due to {h} function. Thus it can not be proven using lpo or mpo.

--- Exercise 82.1
--- 1. Since we want it to be confluent, meaning we want to end up at the same point
---    we can simply make {r1 = f(h(x))} and {r2 = h(h(x))}. This way there is no
---    self reference so it will terminate. It is also confluent, since no matter
---    which path you choose you will end up at the same solution.

---    I have even made a python code to test it, and it worked flawlessly :D
